<p>U.S. federal prosecutors charged Sinaloa Gov. Rubén Rocha Moya and nine other current and former Mexican officials with drug-trafficking and weapons offenses in an indictment unsealed Wednesday in Manhattan federal court. Prosecutors alleged the defendants worked with leaders of the Sinaloa Cartel, including the Los Chapitos faction, to move large quantities of narcotics into the United States in exchange for bribes and political support. The indictment names senior figures in Sinaloa&#x27;s political and law-enforcement systems, including Rocha, Morena Sen. Enrique Inzunza Cázarez, a Culiacán official, state prosecutors and former police commanders. U.S. prosecutors alleged the arrangement allowed cartel members to operate with impunity and involved intimidation, kidnapping and violence tied to the cartel&#x27;s political interests. Rocha denied the accusations, calling them false and politically motivated. Mexico&#x27;s Foreign Ministry questioned the evidence cited in related U.S. extradition materials and objected to the public release of information it said was protected by treaty confidentiality.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/lxwBEzmos1YwGIoPwM0F29AB6fxT7k84a2LuB03srXL4hvOX4kFqigu9sx-L3DsZy_4bXq_O18oLrSaygKJslO_QcIAO8bEPbL8b9o6nWInQ0cktDg_fihQd6ZtBB-XTf89BPek-rUlCPvhD1OrdETAweV7TMXyRVxWxurocCSeUDZ74rsiN6H-dI7FFmJdxnum1ZUBjiU6SRSSp9B1LULBelMZGzH-spO_bFY-oxgVPxixXqPoMRVicOc16HUwSjwVATQNoUa9ir42ENg' alt='Sinaloa state Gov. Rubén Rocha Moya at an annual earthquake drill in Culiacán, Mexico, Sept. 19, 2024.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Possible penalties: Spanish-language accounts citing the indictment said Rocha could face life imprisonment or a mandatory minimum of 40 years if convicted on the U.S. charges.</li><li>DEA source allegation: Fox News reported that the U.S. Attorney&#x27;s Office accused officials of helping torture and murder a DEA source as part of the alleged cartel-protection scheme.</li><li>Cartel designation: The Sinaloa Cartel was among six Mexican drug-trafficking groups designated as foreign terrorist organizations by President Trump&#x27;s administration, according to Le Monde and Mexican coverage.</li></ul><blockquote>The Sinaloa Cartel is a ruthless criminal organization that has flooded this community with dangerous drugs for decades - Jay Clayton</blockquote><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>U.S. Attorney Jay Clayton: Clayton said corrupt politicians and law-enforcement officials allowed the Sinaloa Cartel and similar groups to operate more freely and successfully. His office said the defendants collectively received millions of dollars in drug money. (<a href='https://www.nbcnewyork.com/news/national-international/mexican-governor-officials-charged-smuggle-drugs/6496389/'>NBC New York</a>)</li><li>Rubén Rocha Moya: Rocha rejected the charges and described them as an attack on Mexico&#x27;s governing political movement, not only on him. (<a href='https://www.straitstimes.com/world/us-charges-governor-of-mexicos-sinaloa-state-for-ties-to-cartel'>The Straits Times</a>)</li><li>Mexico&#x27;s Foreign Ministry: The ministry said the case was proceeding under extradition law and that files were sent to Mexico&#x27;s Attorney General&#x27;s Office for review before any Mexican decision on provisional arrests or surrender. (<a href='https://www.foxnews.com/us/us-charges-mexican-governor-top-officials-cartel-conspiracy-mexico-fires-back-proof'>Fox News</a>, <a href='https://www.tvazteca.com/aztecanoticias/podrian-detener-en-mexico-al-gobernador-sinaloa-en-funciones-ruben-moya-por-cargos-en-eu/'>TV Azteca</a>)</li><li>Morena lawmakers: Ricardo Monreal said the defendants retain the presumption of innocence until allegations are proven, while Ignacio Mier expressed support for Rocha and Senator Enrique Inzunza. (<a href='https://lopezdoriga.com/nacional/acusacion-de-ee-uu-contra-gobierno-de-sinaloa-divide-a-oficialismo-y-oposicion-en-mexico/'>López-Dóriga Digital</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3